==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|fit|-ness}}. {{etydate|16th century}}, with the physical sense from 1935.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɪt.nəs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-fitness.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪtnəs|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# The condition of being [[fit]], [[suitable]] or [[appropriate]].
#: {{ant|en|unfitness}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|title=Mountain Charley|author=E. J. Guerin|page=5|text=Having introduced my two characters separately, let me present them together. Why not? He, young, wealthy, honorable; she also young, also intelligent — why not bring them together? There is an eternal '''fitness''' in all things, and nothing more so than in the case of man and woman.}}
# The [[cultivation]] of an attractive and/or healthy physique.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 11, 2011|author=Phil McNulty|title=Liverpool 3 - 0 Man City|work=BBC Sport|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9449111.stm
|passage=Carroll has been edging slowly towards full '''fitness''' after his expensive arrival from Newcastle United and his partnership with £23m Luis Suarez showed rich promise as Liverpool controlled affairs from start to finish.}}
# {{lb|en|evolutionary theory}} An [[organism]]'s or [[species]]' degree of success in finding a [[mate]] and producing [[offspring]].
#: {{syn|en|suitability|capability}}
# {{lb|en|UK|slang}} The condition of being [[attractive]], [[fanciable]] or [[beautiful]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:beautiful}}

